{"text": "Category Q is Learning Disability. The educational description is: A learning disability affects the acquisition, organization, retention, understanding, or use of verbal or nonverbal information in a student who otherwise shows at least average thinking and reasoning abilities. This description is about educational impact and the support required for meaningful participation. It should not be used as a prediction of intelligence, personality, or future potential.\n\nOur fictional student is Amara Patel, who is in Grade 8 and uses she/her pronouns. Amara has a specific learning disability affecting word-level reading, spelling, and written expression. Her oral reasoning and understanding of complex ideas are strong. This background information matters because it connects the designation to observable access needs at school.\n\nWhen we plan for Amara, we ask three practical questions: What can the student already do? What barriers are interfering with participation or learning? And what support will remove those barriers while building independence? Those questions keep the profile individualized and useful. Planning should include the student, family, teacher, case manager, and relevant specialists.\n\nI will now hand the presentation to Presenter 2, who will begin with Amara’s strengths and communication.", "voice": "en-GB-SoniaNeural", "audio": "special-education-Q-02.mp3", "words": [{"text": "Category", "start": 0.1, "end": 0.7, "start_char": 0, "end_char": 8}, {"text": "Q", "start": 0.7125, "end": 1.1375, "start_char": 9, "end_char": 10}, {"text": "is", "start": 1.15, "end": 1.375, "start_char": 11, "end_char": 13}, {"text": "Learning", "start": 1.3875, "end": 1.7625, "start_char": 14, "end_char": 22}, {"text": "Disability", "start": 1.775, "end": 2.5875, "start_char": 23, "end_char": 33}, {"text": "The", "start": 2.95, "end": 3.1375, "start_char": 35, "end_char": 38}, {"text": "educational", "start": 3.15, "end": 3.75, "start_char": 39, "end_char": 50